# Design Binding — html-mockup

`DESIGN.md` `## Screens × form factors` lists component **names** only. The per-element detail
— text labels, badges, hint text, timestamp **format**, app-shell chrome, control order — lives in
the `.html` mockup the table links to. Implementing from the name list alone drops elements
silently (a category badge, a keyboard hint, a header) and nobody notices until a human compares
screens by eye. This skill closes that gap mechanically.

The agent has already read `DESIGN.md`. For every screen a UI task implements (plan time) or has
implemented (implement time), run the three steps below. Read-only on the design source — never
edit files under `docs/design/<id>/`.

## Step 1 — Locate the artifact

In `DESIGN.md § Screens × form factors`, each screen row links to
`docs/design/<EPIC-SEQ>/screens/<screen>-<form>.html`. `Read` that file. One file per screen ×
form factor.

## Step 2 — Build the element manifest

Parse the **populated / default** state only — the `<section>`/`<div>` that is visible (NOT marked
`hidden`, NOT `data-state="loading|empty|error"` unless that IS the screen's default). When a file
ships an in-page state switcher, use the panel the mockup opens by default.

**Exclude mockup-only chrome** — it is explicitly not shippable: `.state-tabs`, `.state-switcher`,
`.mockup-controls`, the reviewer `<nav>`, and any `<script>` toggling states. The skill and the
mockups call these out as "NOT shipped."

From what remains, enumerate every user-visible element, in order:

- text labels + their exact copy (headings, button labels, link text)
- badges / chips / status pills (e.g. a `note-category-badge`)
- hint / helper / placeholder / error text (e.g. a `field-hint`, a keyboard-shortcut line)
- timestamps **and their format** — relative ("2 hours ago") vs absolute ("May 29, 2026"). The
  format is part of the spec; matching the element but not the format is a miss.
- icons + their accessible labels (`aria-label`, visually-hidden text)
- sub-components nested inside a row / card — a `data-component` inside another `data-component`,
  or a child element a later feature added to a component an earlier feature defined (e.g. a
  category badge added to the existing list row)
- app-shell chrome: `<header>`, page `<h1>`/title, nav, toolbar buttons + their labels
- control order within a group (e.g. Save before Cancel, or the editor's Title → Body → Category →
  Save → Cancel tab order)

## Step 3 — Diff against the code + flag

For each manifest element, confirm the implemented component renders it (or, at plan time, that a
task in the breakdown covers it). For any element present in the manifest but absent from the code
**and** not already in the task scope / PLAN.md file plan, append to
`docs/features/$ARGUMENTS/FLAGS.md` (create if absent):

```
### AF-<next>: inconsistency · task: T-NN · <code-file>
design element "<element>" on screen <S> present in html-mockup artifact but not implemented — DESIGN.md component list omitted it.
```

`<next>` is the next sequential AF id. The flag is triaged at `/arh-human-review` before commit-PR.

Do not silently skip an element because the component "looks done." A missing badge / hint / header
is a flag, not a judgment call.

## Contract (uniform across all design providers)

- **Input:** `DESIGN.md` (already read) + the provider artifact for each screen.
- **Output:** zero or more `AF-NN` flags; the UI verified element-complete against the artifact.
- **Read-only** on the design source. Never weaken: a missing element is always a flag.

